  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 332 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nA parade of convectively enhanced mid-level impulses continues \nto drive waves of convection early this morning as they track \neast-northeastward along the northern periphery of the \nsubtropical ridge parked over the Southeast. Most of the \nconvection overnight has been focused mainly south of the I-80 \ncorridor from northern MO eastward into west-central \nIL\u2014essentially along the northern terminus of a ~30kt \nsouthwesterly low-level jet (per area VAD wind profiles). While \nshowers and storms have been more widely scattered in our area \nthus far early this morning, a wave of more widespread showers \nand storms is quickly moving into western sections of our area \nas of this writing. The primary threat of thunderstorms and very\nheavy rainfall will remain south of I-80 with this wave through\naround daybreak. Farther north, the Chicago metro area will \nreside along the northern periphery of this wave, though a \nperiod of lighter rain remains likely through daybreak, \nparticularly across southern sections of the metro.\n\nFollowing this initial early morning period of showers and \nstorms, chances for scattered showers may persist at times \nthrough the day as remnant upstream convectively enhanced \nimpulses track eastward into the lower Great Lakes. However, the\noverall threat and coverage of thunderstorms during the day \nshould remain low, with the main chances this afternoon staying \nto our south closer to the better MUCAPE. Cloudy skies and \nnortherly surface flow will result in another cool early-autumn \nday, particularly along and north of I-80 where temperatures \nwill once again remain in the lower 70s. Farther south into \ncentral portions of IL and IN, closer to the surface frontal \nboundary, a warm and humid afternoon is expected.\n\nAnother round of heavy rain-producing showers and thunderstorms\nis likely to develop tonight into early Thursday morning, \nthough not everyone will be impacted by the heavy rain. Instead,\na corridor of eastward-training storms is likely to develop \nlater this evening along the nose of an intensifying \nsouthwesterly low-level jet. Initially, it appears this corridor\nof storms will focus across eastern IA. However, as the low-\nlevel jet veers overnight, the focus may expand east-\nsoutheastward into portions of our area. These setups are always\ntricky, as forecast guidance rarely depicts the precise \nlocation of the heaviest rain axis and frequently places it too \nfar north. With this in mind, current thinking is that the \nheaviest rainfall axis may remain largely south of the I-80 \ncorridor, as depicted by the 00Z HREF. Wherever this favored \ncorridor for training storms sets up late tonight into early \nThursday morning, heavy rainfall could result in localized \ninstances of flash flooding.\n\nA period of drier weather is expected Thursday afternoon through\nat least the first half of Friday as surface high pressure\nbuilding into the Upper Great Lakes briefly pushes the surface\nfrontal boundary well south of our area. East-northeasterly\nsurface winds will yield seasonable temperatures in the 70s on\nboth Thursday and Friday. Shower and storm chances will return\nlate Friday as the next notable shortwave ejects from a\nSouthwest trough across the western Great Lakes. At this point,\nthe best shower and storm chances late Friday into Friday night\nlook to remain largely across WI, extending possibly as far\nsouth as portions of far northern IL (north of I-88). Better\nchances will return to most of our area over the weekend as the\nnext cold front sags southward into the area. Continued very\nhigh atmospheric column moisture (precipitable water values near\n2\") will also bring another threat of locally heavy rainfall\nwith these weekend storms.\n\nKJB",
